  "salt": "E3Loolm_N8wk_7sKX5uGWg==",
  "text": "The Celts counted five directions, not four. The center joined north, south, east, and west, and it moved with the speaker, marking both place and idea. The Irish split into five provinces, with Leinster east, Munster southwest, Connacht west, and Ulster north. The fifth province was the movable center, fixed later as Mide or Meath. Each province kept a center such as Cruachan, Knockaulin, Emain Macha, or Knockainy. The whole island had two centers, the hills of Uisneach and Tara, and Uisneach held the Stone of Divisions, a boulder shaped like a map of Ireland. Deosil meant moving clockwise with the Sun, tuathal meant anticlockwise, and neo-pagans still call widdershins unlucky in a ritual circle.",
  "objective": "Explain the five directions and the deosil-tuathal pair in Celtic thought.",
